# The ledger of `xlsx`: what Go's excelize (github.com/xuri/excelize/v2
# 2.11.1), Python's openpyxl (3.1.5) and Rust's calamine (0.38.0, a reader)
# and rust_xlsxwriter (0.102.0, a writer) let a program do with a
# spreadsheet of Office Open XML, one block per capability, and what Rebel
# answers. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-18: `go doc -all` on
# excelize, `dir()` of openpyxl's modules and classes from its wheel, and the
# items `cargo doc` wrote for both crates. None of the three languages'
# standard libraries reads or writes a spreadsheet.
#
# The workbooks the tests read were made by openpyxl 3.1.5 and excelize
# 2.11.1, and the values expected are what openpyxl reads from them. The
# differential of 2026-11-18 had openpyxl write 330 random workbooks (text of
# every plane, numbers of every size, booleans, dates, formulas) that this
# package read, and this package write 330 that openpyxl read: of 23,432
# cells, every one agreed but 88, each one of the two disagreements written
# below (`_xHHHH_` read, and a serial below 0 kept a number); excelize opened
# all 300 workbooks of the second half. 2,000 workbooks with a part mutated
# were read plain and 500 more built with `--sanitize`, and every one was
# read or failed with a code.

capability xlsx/open  a workbook read from a file, a stream that can seek, or bytes in memory
  heroes  xlsx.load, xlsx.load_with, xlsx.read, xlsx.read_bytes
  go      excelize.OpenFile, excelize.OpenReader, File.ReadZipReader, excelize.Options, File.Close, File.CharsetTranscoder
  rust    calamine::open_workbook, open_workbook_from_rs, Xlsx::new, Reader::new, trait.Reader, trait.ReaderRef
  python  openpyxl.load_workbook, openpyxl.open, openpyxl.reader, Workbook.close, Workbook.path, Workbook.read_only, Workbook.data_only
  note    a stream must seek, since a zip is read from its end, as calamine's Read + Seek; bytes in memory go through a temporary file the call removes; formulas and their values are both kept, so openpyxl's data_only and read_only modes have nothing to choose

capability xlsx/bounded  a hostile file refused within bounds the caller sets: a zip bomb, an XML bomb, too many cells
  heroes  xlsx.ReadOptions, xlsx.default_read_options, xlsx.DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES, xlsx.DEFAULT_MAX_CELLS
  go      Options.UnzipSizeLimit, Options.UnzipXMLSizeLimit, ErrOptionsUnzipSizeLimit
  rust    -
  python  openpyxl.DEFUSEDXML
  note    one bound counts every part decompressed and every cell's text, so a shared string named by a million cells is counted a million times; excelize's bound is 16 GB of parts by default, here 64 MiB; an entity declared in a part is refused by `xml`, which is what defusedxml adds to openpyxl

capability xlsx/sheets  the sheets of a workbook, in order, found by name, with their visibility and the sheet shown first
  heroes  xlsx.Workbook (sheets, active), xlsx.Sheet, xlsx.Visibility, xlsx.sheet_of, xlsx.sheet_names
  go      File.GetSheetList, File.GetSheetMap, File.GetSheetName, File.GetSheetIndex, File.GetSheetVisible, File.GetActiveSheetIndex, ErrSheetNotExist
  rust    Reader.sheet_names, Reader.sheets_metadata, Reader.worksheets, Sheet.name, Sheet.typ, Sheet.visible, SheetVisible, SheetType, Sheets (the enum), Xlsx.metadata, Reader.metadata, Sheets.metadata
  python  Workbook.sheetnames, Workbook.worksheets, Workbook.get_sheet_by_name, Workbook.get_sheet_names, Workbook.index, Workbook.get_index, Workbook.active, Worksheet.title, Worksheet.sheet_state, Worksheet.SHEETSTATE_VISIBLE, SHEETSTATE_HIDDEN, SHEETSTATE_VERYHIDDEN
  note    a name is found byte for byte, as openpyxl finds it, where excelize ignores the case; a chart sheet holds no cell and is not read (`xlsx/charts`)

capability xlsx/values  a cell's value read with its type: number, text from the shared strings or inline, boolean, error
  heroes  xlsx.Value, xlsx.Cell, xlsx.cell_at, xlsx.value_at, xlsx.format_value
  go      File.GetCellValue, File.GetCellType, excelize.CellType, excelize.Cell, Options.RawCellValue
  rust    calamine::Data, DataRef, DataType (every is_ and get_ and as_ method), CellErrorType, Range.get_value, Range.get, Cell, Cell.get_position, Cell.get_value, Data.Bool, Data.Float, Data.Int, Data.String, Data.Empty, Data.Error, Data.get_bool, get_datetime, get_datetime_iso, get_duration_iso, get_error, get_float, get_int, get_string, as_i64, as_string, as_f64, is_bool, is_datetime_iso, is_duration_iso, is_error, is_float, is_int, is_string, CellErrorType.Div0, NA, Name, Null, Num, Ref, Value, GettingData
  python  Cell.value, Cell.data_type, Cell.internal_value, Cell.check_error, Cell.check_string, Worksheet.cell, Worksheet.__getitem__
  note    an error is its text, `#N/A`; an ISO 8601 cell (`t="d"`) reads as a date; `_xHHHH_` escapes are read, as ECMA-376 and calamine read them, where openpyxl keeps them as written; a number that is a NaN or an infinity in a file is refused `invalid`; `format_value` writes a value as plain text, as excelize's GetCellValue with RawCellValue, the number format left to `xlsx/display`

capability xlsx/rows  a sheet's values as rows, every row as long as the widest, empty cells as empty values
  heroes  xlsx.rows_of, xlsx.Sheet (cells)
  go      File.GetRows, File.Rows, Rows.Next, Rows.Columns, Rows.Error, Rows.Close, Rows.GetRowOpts, File.GetCols, File.Cols, Cols.Next, Cols.Rows, Cols.Error
  rust    Reader.worksheet_range, worksheet_range_at, worksheet_range_ref, worksheet_range_at_ref, Range.rows, Range.cells, Range.used_cells, Range.start, Range.end, Range.width, Range.height, Range.get_size, Range.is_empty, Range.headers, Range.range, Range.new, Range.empty, Range.from_sparse, Range.set_value, Rows, Cells, UsedCells, Reader.with_header_row, HeaderRow
  python  Worksheet.iter_rows, Worksheet.iter_cols, Worksheet.rows, Worksheet.columns, Worksheet.values
  note    the rows start at `A1` and every row is as long as the widest, as openpyxl's iter_rows, where calamine's range starts at the first cell used; a `limit` on the values, since two cells far apart would make seventeen thousand million

capability xlsx/dates  a number whose format is a date read as a date, by the workbook's 1900 or 1904 epoch
  heroes  xlsx.Value (date), xlsx.Epoch, xlsx.Workbook (epoch), xlsx.is_date_format
  go      excelize.ExcelDateToTime, File.GetWorkbookProps (Date1904)
  rust    calamine::ExcelDateTime, ExcelDateTime.as_f64, is_datetime, is_duration, to_ymd_hms_milli, ExcelDateTimeType, Data.DateTime, Data.DateTimeIso, Data.DurationIso, Xlsx.has_1904_epoch
  python  openpyxl.utils.datetime.from_excel, CALENDAR_WINDOWS_1900, CALENDAR_MAC_1904, WINDOWS_EPOCH, MAC_EPOCH, SECS_PER_DAY, days_to_time, Workbook.epoch, Workbook.excel_base_date, Cell.is_date, Cell.base_date
  note    a moment is rounded to the millisecond, as openpyxl (45000.123456789 is 02:57:46.667), where excelize rounds to the second; a serial below 1 is a time on 1899-12-30, as excelize, where openpyxl answers a time with no day; a serial below 0 stays a number, where openpyxl reads a day before 1900; 60 is 1900-02-28 in both; an elapsed format, `[h]:mm`, leaves a number of days, where openpyxl answers a timedelta and calamine a duration, since a `Value` has no duration

capability xlsx/serials  a date turned into Excel's serial number and back, by either epoch
  heroes  xlsx.serial_of, xlsx.date_of
  go      excelize.ExcelDateToTime
  rust    rust_xlsxwriter::ExcelDateTime, ExcelDateTime.from_serial_datetime, to_excel, to_excel_serial_date, from_ymd, from_hms, from_hms_milli, and_hms, and_hms_milli, from_timestamp, parse_from_str, utc_datetime, IntoExcelDateTime, IntoCustomDateTime
  python  openpyxl.utils.datetime.to_excel, from_excel, time_to_days, timedelta_to_days, to_ISO8601, from_ISO8601, ISO_FORMAT, ISO_REGEX, ISO_DURATION
  note    a date before 1900-01-01 (1904-01-01) has no serial and fails `unsupported`, but the day zero, 1899-12-30, which a time alone stands on; openpyxl writes 1899-12-31 as 0 as well, refused here

capability xlsx/formulas-read  a cell's formula read as text, beside the value Excel last computed for it, a shared formula's text for every cell it covers
  heroes  xlsx.Cell (formula, value)
  go      File.GetCellFormula
  rust    Reader.worksheet_formula, XlsxCellFormula, XlsxCellFormulaMetadataRecord, XlsxFormulaMetadata, calamine::expand_shared_formula, expand_shared_formula_into, XlsxCellReader.next_formula, next_cell_with_formula, next_cell_with_formula_metadata
  python  Cell.value (a text opening with `=`), load_workbook's data_only, openpyxl.formula, openpyxl.formula.translate.Translator, openpyxl.utils.FORMULAE
  note    a shared formula is given its text at every cell it covers, its references moved as Excel moves them, as calamine's expand_shared_formula and openpyxl's Translator; an array formula is read as the text of its first cell

capability xlsx/merged  the merged areas of a sheet read
  heroes  xlsx.Sheet (merged), xlsx.Area
  go      File.GetMergeCells, MergeCell, MergeCell.GetCellValue, MergeCell.GetStartAxis, MergeCell.GetEndAxis
